pseudo-Boolean proof version 3.0
% refused-at: parse
% Regression test (wrong verdict): the DIMACS CNF header parser read its two
% counts and dropped the rest of the line.
%
% Clause data in DIMACS is a stream -- clauses may legally share a line and
% span lines -- but the `p cnf <vars> <clauses>` header is not clause data, and
% this file's header line carries the clause `1 0` after the counts. Under a
% line-oriented reading the header is malformed and the file must be refused;
% under the stream reading the clauses are {x1}, {~x1}, {~x1}, three where the
% header declares two, so the count check must refuse it. Under NEITHER reading
% is the file satisfiable-as-declared. The parser invented a third reading:
% `parse_header` returned after two integers, the trailing clause vanished, the
% formula became {~x1}, {~x1} -- satisfiable -- and the declared count of 2
% matched the 2 clauses that survived, so nothing caught it.
%
% `sol ~x1` then verified an unsatisfiable file as `s VERIFIED SATISFIABLE` at
% exit 0 under default flags. The independent second reader was written to the
% same shape and inherited the same lenience, so `--kernel-verify` reported
% "2 formula constraint(s) cross-checked against the file on disk". The same
% one-item-per-line class as wrong verdict 25, in the one DIMACS line that is
% line-oriented.
%
% Found by a ninth red team, from a probe left in flight when it was cut off;
% the header line was its last experiment.
f 2;
sol ~x1;
output NONE;
conclusion SAT : ~x1;
end pseudo-Boolean proof;
